F_n^(4)(x) - F_4(x) = N(n,x) / D(n)
  D(n) = n**3*(n - 3)*(n - 2)*(n - 1)
  N(n,x) = n**5*(-6*x**8 + 8*x**7 + 6*x**6 - 12*x**5 + 6*x**4 - 6*x**2 + 4*x) + n**4*(11*x**8 - 66*x**6 + 62*x**5 - 5*x**4 - 8*x**3 + 36*x**2 - 30*x) + n**3*(-6*x**8 + 24*x**6 + 20*x**5 - 76*x**4 + 42*x**3 - 78*x**2 + 74*x) + n**2*(11*x**4 - 50*x**3 + 67*x**2 - 52*x) + n*(-28*x**3 + 58*x**2 - 30*x) + 12*x**2 - 12*x

  |coefficient of n^5 as poly in x| <= 48 (sum of |x-coeffs|)
  |coefficient of n^4 as poly in x| <= 218 (sum of |x-coeffs|)
  |coefficient of n^3 as poly in x| <= 320 (sum of |x-coeffs|)
  |coefficient of n^2 as poly in x| <= 180 (sum of |x-coeffs|)
  |coefficient of n^1 as poly in x| <= 116 (sum of |x-coeffs|)
  |coefficient of n^0 as poly in x| <= 24 (sum of |x-coeffs|)

  sum of all C_d = 906

For n>=1: |N(n,x)| <= sum_d C_d * n^d <= (sum C_d) * n^{deg} = 906 * n^ 5  (since n^d<=n^deg for d<=deg, n>=1)

D(n) = n^3(n-1)(n-2)(n-3).  For n>=6: (n-1)>=n/2, (n-2)>=n/2,
(n-3)>=n/2, so D(n) >= n^3*(n/2)^3 = n^6/8.

=> |F_n^(4)(x)-F_4(x)| <= 906*n^5 / (n^6/8) = 7248/n  for n>=6, x in [0,1].

==============================================================================
Corollary D4.5 (PROVED): for every n>=6 and every x in [0,1],
  |F_n^(4)(x) - F_4(x)| <= 7248/n
==============================================================================

Numeric cross-check, n=6..3000: worst observed ratio |gap|/(7248/n) = 0.0001 at n=3000, x=0.3698 (must stay <=1 for the bound to hold; comfortably inside).

Leading-order 1/n term (NOT itself proved as a uniform finite-n
bound; asymptotic only, disclosed honestly, matching D2.5/D3.5's
own two-tier disclosure):
  g1(x) [coefficient of 1/n as n->oo] = -6*x**8 + 8*x**7 + 6*x**6 - 12*x**5 + 6*x**4 - 6*x**2 + 4*x
  max_x |g1(x)| on [0,1] = 0.708718 at x = 0.369888
  (crude proved constant 7248 vs. sharp asymptotic leading constant ~0.7087 -- large gap, not optimized here,
  same honest disclosure as D2.5 (~0.71 vs 12) and D3.5 (~0.71 vs 22))
